  [1mThe fork:[0m whether change is detected on one person's machine or on a server. This decides nearly everything downstream
  and is expensive to reverse.

  - [1mClient-only (localStorage, one browser):[0m cheapest by far, no backend, works today as a static page. Notifications
    are just in-page toasts plus an unread list. Buys you a working feature this week; rules out notifying anyone who
    isn't looking at that tab on that device, and rules out "someone else changed your task" entirely — because there is
    no someone else.
  - [1mShared backend (API + DB + real accounts):[0m the only version where "users" is plural and a task can change while
    you're away. Buys real multi-user notifications, delivery outside the tab (email/push), and durable read state;
    costs a server, auth, a schema, and deployment — this is most of the work, and the notification piece is the small
    part on top.
  - [1mClient-only now, backend-shaped later:[0m build the local version but keep an event log and a delivery interface as
    seams. Cheap now, and the data model survives the move; the seams cost a little ceremony that's wasted if you never
    go multi-user.

  [1mMy recommendation: the third.[0m Your phrasing ("users", "tasks they care about") describes the multi-user system, but
  there's no app here to attach it to yet — building auth and a server before there's a single task on the page inverts
  the order. Getting the event and subscription model right locally is the part that carries over; the transport is
  replaceable.
